  "articleBody": "Planning a kitchen bump-out or major addition requires careful scheduling and trade management. Here are frequent missteps homeowners make before construction gets rolling.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n- Designing cabinets before confirming structural engineering: Finalizing cabinetry sizes before verifying bearing walls or beam sizes leads to costly order changes.\n- Starting work without required building permits: Altering exterior walls, gas lines, or electrical circuits triggers local code reviews; building without permits causes stop-work orders.\n- Underestimating electrical and ventilation demands: Modern appliances and heavy-duty range hoods need dedicated circuits and proper exterior venting paths planned early.\n- Moving gas lines or plumbing blindly: Relocating sink lines or gas ranges without checking floor joist directions creates routing headaches.\n- Closing walls before rough inspections pass: Concealing plumbing, mechanical lines, or wiring before building officials sign off forces expensive drywall tear-outs.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options\nCheck that your contractor's quote includes clear line items for trade coordination, permit scheduling, and rough inspections alongside finish carpentry and drywall.\n\nFor open communication and expert carpentry on your upcoming kitchen addition, contact HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
